WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.534 --> 00:00:03.467
-We're back with Hilary Duff.
This is very exciting.

00:00:03.467 --> 00:00:05.200
-Below 60.
-Yeah.

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:06.701
-What would you
describe these as?

00:00:06.701 --> 00:00:09.100
In-home fragrances?
-Yes I would.

00:00:09.100 --> 00:00:11.033
I would say
they're in-home fragrances.

00:00:11.033 --> 00:00:12.534
They're plug-ins.
-They're plug-ins.

00:00:12.534 --> 00:00:15.434
-You know, we all kind of grew
up with said plug-in.

00:00:15.434 --> 00:00:18.200
-Yes.
-They are updated, my friend.

00:00:18.200 --> 00:00:20.367
-And you are the chief
brand director

00:00:20.367 --> 00:00:21.701
for these fragrances.

00:00:21.701 --> 00:00:23.467
-Which basically means
I get to be really bossy with

00:00:23.467 --> 00:00:25.434
all the boys
that started the company.

00:00:25.434 --> 00:00:26.734
-Oh, that's very nice.
-Yeah.

00:00:26.734 --> 00:00:29.000
And I get to tell them
like, "I don't like that.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:31.000
No, that sticks out
too far from the wall.

00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:32.934
Change this, change that.
Oh, what about this scent?

00:00:32.934 --> 00:00:34.501
Oh, that scent.
I like this thing together."

00:00:34.501 --> 00:00:36.634
-And you seem like a very
fun person to work with.

00:00:36.634 --> 00:00:38.667
[ Laughter ]
-It's a lot of texts.

00:00:38.667 --> 00:00:40.300
It's a lot of rapid-fire texts.
-Oh, okay.

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:41.801
So it's all on texts.
-No, no, no.

00:00:41.801 --> 00:00:43.133
We have meetings,
too, of course.

00:00:43.133 --> 00:00:44.667
But, no, they're great.

00:00:44.667 --> 00:00:46.467
-So, a lot of it
is how the product looks,

00:00:46.467 --> 00:00:47.901
but a lot of it
must be the scents

00:00:47.901 --> 00:00:49.534
and then also
the names of the scents.

00:00:49.534 --> 00:00:50.934
First of all,
before I get to the names,

00:00:50.934 --> 00:00:52.367
do you feel like you have
a good nose for this?

00:00:52.367 --> 00:00:54.200
Have you been told over
the years that you do?

00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:55.434
-Yeah.
-Okay.

00:00:55.434 --> 00:00:57.567
-I mean, by the way,
I'm always complimented by

00:00:57.567 --> 00:01:00.067
like, the way
that I smell, so...

00:01:00.067 --> 00:01:02.334
-Like, not by the way
you smell other things,

00:01:02.334 --> 00:01:03.434
but the way
you actually smell.

00:01:03.434 --> 00:01:04.968
-I smell other things
pretty well, too,

00:01:04.968 --> 00:01:06.300
but that's because
I've been obsessed with

00:01:06.300 --> 00:01:07.901
perfume and scents
for a long time.

00:01:07.901 --> 00:01:09.334
-That's very --
So, you've been a --

00:01:09.334 --> 00:01:11.133
I know those kind of people
that they walk in,

00:01:11.133 --> 00:01:13.133
and everybody's like,
"Wow, you smell great."

00:01:13.133 --> 00:01:15.234
That's happened to you
your whole life?

00:01:15.234 --> 00:01:17.167
-Since I was a toddler, yeah.

00:01:17.167 --> 00:01:18.801
[ Laughter ]

00:01:18.801 --> 00:01:21.834
No, but since I started
buying perfume, I guess,

00:01:21.834 --> 00:01:24.434
ever since the Cucumber Melon
from Bath &amp; Body Works, guys.

00:01:24.434 --> 00:01:25.701
-I was...
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:01:25.701 --> 00:01:26.968
-People know.

00:01:26.968 --> 00:01:28.701
But that -- Well, I'm glad
you made the distinction,

00:01:28.701 --> 00:01:29.901
because you're not saying,

00:01:29.901 --> 00:01:31.934
"Look, there's no one
could ever smell like me.

00:01:31.934 --> 00:01:33.200
It comes from within."

00:01:33.200 --> 00:01:35.467
You're basically saying
it is product-based.

00:01:35.467 --> 00:01:37.801
-It's both, Seth. It's both.
-It's both.

00:01:37.801 --> 00:01:39.601
You've got to have
a base coat of just being,

00:01:39.601 --> 00:01:40.901
like, a good-smelling person.

00:01:40.901 --> 00:01:42.200
-Yes. Yes.
-Yeah.

00:01:42.200 --> 00:01:43.567
-I'm sweating.
[ Laughter ]

00:01:46.067 --> 00:01:48.000
-And by the way, the more --
Can I just say?

00:01:48.000 --> 00:01:49.100
The more you're smelling.

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:51.400
Yeah, it's, like --
It's outstanding.

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:53.100
[ Laughter ]

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:54.434
Alright, so, the names.

00:01:54.434 --> 00:01:57.868
Are you coming up with
these names for these scents?

00:01:57.868 --> 00:02:02.033
-Yeah. So, the product
is very, like, universal.

00:02:02.033 --> 00:02:05.534
It can look great in any home.
It's really modern and updated.

00:02:05.534 --> 00:02:07.067
And I was like,
"We got to give the --"

00:02:07.067 --> 00:02:08.901
I mean, I'm the shopper
in my household.

00:02:08.901 --> 00:02:11.200
I'm sure Alexi is the same.
-100%.

00:02:11.200 --> 00:02:13.734
-She's the consumer.
We're buying for the house.

00:02:13.734 --> 00:02:16.267
We're the ones
that get ad-targeted.

00:02:16.267 --> 00:02:19.868
-Yeah.
-They get me every time.

00:02:19.868 --> 00:02:21.501
And I was like,
"We got to hook people in.

00:02:21.501 --> 00:02:23.734
Like, what is --
Let's give this a story."

00:02:23.734 --> 00:02:26.234
Let's give these names like --
Let's be very playful with it."

00:02:26.234 --> 00:02:29.601
Also, the scents last a month,
so, you know, when you're done

00:02:29.601 --> 00:02:33.534
with it, you, like, refill
a cartridge with something else.

00:02:33.534 --> 00:02:35.667
And it's just, like,
constantly evolving,

00:02:35.667 --> 00:02:36.901
which is kind of my personality.

00:02:36.901 --> 00:02:38.067
I'm always wanting
to try something new

00:02:38.067 --> 00:02:39.133
and do something new.

00:02:39.133 --> 00:02:40.200
So, yeah,
the names are very fun.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:41.634
-I'm gonna read you some.
-Okay.

00:02:41.634 --> 00:02:43.601
-If Citrus Were a Feeling.
-Mm-hmm.

00:02:43.601 --> 00:02:45.734
-That's a very unique name
for a smell.

00:02:45.734 --> 00:02:47.901
-Okay, are you judging
or do you like it?

00:02:47.901 --> 00:02:49.267
[ Laughter ]

00:02:49.267 --> 00:02:51.200
-I want you to take
"unique" home with you

00:02:51.200 --> 00:02:53.367
and decide
what you think it means.

00:02:53.367 --> 00:02:55.367
No, I like it,
but I like the better one.

00:02:55.367 --> 00:02:57.267
I like the second one more.
-Okay.

00:02:57.267 --> 00:02:59.267
-No, I'm going to put
my favorite one third.

00:02:59.267 --> 00:03:01.000
Then Mint Disco on Ice.
-Mm-hmm.

00:03:01.000 --> 00:03:03.501
-I like Mint Disco on Ice.
-Okay. You know what?

00:03:03.501 --> 00:03:05.467
The men love that one.
-Do they really?

00:03:05.467 --> 00:03:07.234
-Yeah. I don't know why men
love, like, mint

00:03:07.234 --> 00:03:09.133
and, like, fresh
and, like, cooling and pine

00:03:09.133 --> 00:03:10.334
and things like that.

00:03:10.334 --> 00:03:11.567
-Well, now I feel
like you're judging.

00:03:11.567 --> 00:03:13.901
-Well, I'm a little
judgmental of that one.

00:03:13.901 --> 00:03:16.334
I think it's a little more
geared towards men, but...

00:03:16.334 --> 00:03:19.133
-Gotcha. Alright.
-I love it. I still love it.

00:03:19.133 --> 00:03:21.334
-Oh, nobody believes that.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:24.400 --> 00:03:26.067
-Okay, Seth.
-But here's the winner.

00:03:26.067 --> 00:03:28.701
Vanilla Buys a Timeshare
in Paradise.

00:03:28.701 --> 00:03:30.167
I mean, come on.
That is...

00:03:30.167 --> 00:03:33.033
-Don't you want to buy it?
-That I want to buy.

00:03:33.033 --> 00:03:35.100
And I don't even --
Like, vanilla would not be --

00:03:35.100 --> 00:03:36.667
is not, like,
a top-three scent of mine,

00:03:36.667 --> 00:03:39.234
but I have so much appreciation
for how that --

00:03:39.234 --> 00:03:41.567
Now I'm using it in
the best way I possibly can.

00:03:41.567 --> 00:03:43.200
That is incredibly unique.

00:03:43.200 --> 00:03:45.267
A.I. would never come up with

00:03:45.267 --> 00:03:47.000
Vanilla Buys
a Timeshare in Paradise.

00:03:47.000 --> 00:03:48.367
-Thank you! I agree.

00:03:48.367 --> 00:03:50.234
Some things
still need that human touch.

00:03:50.234 --> 00:03:51.901
-And, so, when --
And do these just sort of --

00:03:51.901 --> 00:03:53.234
I mean, I'm sure
it's a whole team,

00:03:53.234 --> 00:03:54.767
but do you, like --
Do you, like, smell it

00:03:54.767 --> 00:03:56.367
and then go into,
like, a fugue state

00:03:56.367 --> 00:03:58.534
and then you go, "Vanilla
Buys a Timeshare in Paradise"?

00:03:58.534 --> 00:03:59.968
-It just --
Yeah, it just comes to me.

00:03:59.968 --> 00:04:02.234
I start, like, shaking
and I'm like...

00:04:02.234 --> 00:04:05.100
-You just, like, lie in
a milk bath until --

00:04:05.100 --> 00:04:06.567
And then they, like,
plug you in.

00:04:06.567 --> 00:04:10.200
-They've infused the room
so I can, like, feel it deeply.

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:12.367
We do so many, like,
walk-throughs,

00:04:12.367 --> 00:04:14.267
where we have, like,
multiple spaces

00:04:14.267 --> 00:04:17.534
and the sensor plugged in and
they're warm and they're going

00:04:17.534 --> 00:04:19.968
and we all are kind of a part
of this process

00:04:19.968 --> 00:04:22.100
together of creating the names.

00:04:22.100 --> 00:04:24.167
I love that you are
really interested

00:04:24.167 --> 00:04:25.534
in the creation of the name.

00:04:25.534 --> 00:04:28.667
So much more goes behind it.
-I'm very impressed by it.

00:04:28.667 --> 00:04:30.100
And congratulations on that.

00:04:30.100 --> 00:04:31.601
Congratulations on the book.
-Thank you.

00:04:31.601 --> 00:04:33.267
-And congratulations on --
I'm sure it's going to be

00:04:33.267 --> 00:04:35.534
the best Christmas card
of anyone I know.

00:04:35.534 --> 00:04:37.567
-I'm gonna send you one.
-You better. You better.

00:04:37.567 --> 00:04:39.400
We'll send you one.
Hilary Duff, everyone.

00:04:39.400 --> 00:04:42.067
Her new book,
"My Little Sweet Boy,"

00:04:42.067 --> 00:04:43.334
is on sale everywhere now,

00:04:43.334 --> 00:04:44.501
but please support local
and independent bookstores.

00:04:44.501 --> 00:04:45.667
And stick around.

00:04:45.667 --> 00:04:48.000
We'll be right back with
Please Don't Destroy.

